      --reconcile-after-restore runs ONE post-restore reconciliation pass and exits (#551): after
      restoring metadata from a backup, with the writers STOPPED. It marks fragments the restore
      stranded so GC can reclaim them, and reports chunks whose bytes were already reclaimed before
      the restore resurrected their maps. It marks; it never deletes.
      --gc-expired-pending additionally lets the GC pass reclaim expired pending-lease garbage
      (a crashed write's fan-out). UNSAFE while ANY writer is running against this metadata
      backend: the CLI stamps leases from a logical clock, so an in-flight write's lease already
      reads as expired and its bytes would be swept mid-write (#557). Pass it only when you can
      attest the backend is taking no writes; without it GC still reclaims delete/overwrite orphans.
      --reap-abandoned-uploads runs ONE multipart reaper pass and exits (#625): metadata-only, needs no --endpoints; exits non-zero when a session is held, a fence's outcome is unknown or the profile check refuses.
      It is refused with --endpoints/--ids/--failure-domains or --gc-expired-pending (it would ignore them),
      and on a redb --data-dir that holds no metadata store (it creates none). Its last line names the store it ran on.
  wyrd s3 --access-key KEY --secret-key SECRET [--s3-listen ADDR] [--data-dir DIR] [--chunk-size N] [--region NAME] [--endpoints URL,URL,…] [--metadata-backend redb|tikv|fdb] [--coordination-backend mem|etcd] [--otlp-endpoint URL]
  wyrd demo

  every command also accepts:
  [--log-level DIRECTIVE] [--log-format json|text]
  Structured logs go to stderr (stdout stays the payload stream). The level
  defaults to `info`, or $RUST_LOG when set; --log-level overrides both.

  --endpoints drives a local distributed cluster: fragments fan out over gRPC
  to the listed D servers (metadata held locally). See README "Run a local cluster".

  custodian: --endpoints wires the reconstruction plane over the D-server fleet.
  When --endpoints is given, --ids and --failure-domains are REQUIRED and must
  each list one entry per endpoint (matching each D-server's own --id /
  --failure-domain); the role never fabricates identity or topology from endpoint
  order. Omit all three to run the leader-elected role with no reconstruction plane.

  s3: --chunk-size N sets the bytes per chunk (default 1 MiB). Accepted: 1048576 to 16777216
  (1 to 16 MiB): the floor keeps a single PUT's flat chunk map inside the metadata
  value ceiling, the ceiling is set by the D-server gRPC message limit.
  The chunk size c sets the object size limit: one PUT holds at most 158 x c bytes
  (158 MiB at 1 MiB, about 2.47 GiB at 16 MiB); a larger PUT is refused EntityTooLarge.
  Multipart upload is not supported yet. Once it is, a part will hold up to 158 x c
  bytes and an object up to 80896 x c in at most 10000 parts (about 79 GiB at 1 MiB,
  1.23 TiB at 16 MiB). Until then, a larger --chunk-size is the way to accept
  larger objects.

